This is the source code for the Firefox snap[1].

Builds are in Launchpad[2].

For merge requests, please target the nightly channel if you have no particular
reason to do otherwise.

[1]https://snapcraft.io/firefox
[2]https://launchpad.net/~mozilla-snaps/+snaps

Firefox MSRV monitor and compiler-update proposals
--------------------------------------------------

From the repository root, with Python 3.11+, PyYAML and dpkg available:

    python3 -B .github/scripts/check-rust-msrv.py

The command reads mozilla-central declarations through Mozilla's official GitHub
main branch, pins the nightly snap recipe, records published-nightly source
context, and checks official Ubuntu Noble Rust/Cargo publications for amd64 and
arm64 when a compiler shortfall exists. It never uses the runner's installed
compiler or executes downloaded source, build steps or compiler helpers.

The JSON outcome is no-update-needed, compiler-available, publication-gap, or
unknown. Exit status 0 means the conclusion was established (including an actual
publication gap); status 1 means evidence was incomplete or failed. The report
includes revisions, publication links, per-architecture versions and timestamped
response hashes. A family lower bound is not a claim about an installed patch
version, and package publication is not a successful installation/build test.
Backports results are restricted to the arm64 components configured by the
recipe. Full upstream MSRV suitability is checked before Debian ordering selects
the highest eligible revision; observed older packages remain visible as evidence.

Mutable metadata responses must be at most one hour old, allowing five minutes
of future clock skew. History/pagination and response sizes are bounded; reaching
a bound yields unknown, never a package-absence claim. GITHUB_TOKEN is optional
for GitHub API rate limits and is not forwarded to other hosts. No token is
needed for anonymous public reads. The per-request timeout defaults to 30 seconds
and can be changed with --timeout.

By default (or explicitly with --dry-run), this command only reads HTTPS resources
and prints its JSON report. When a compiler is available, proposal.diff and
proposal.body preview the coordinated recipe update and PR explanation. The
editor changes only existing dump-syms Cargo selections/invocation, Firefox's
Rust/Cargo packages and its compiler/helper selector. Qualifiers, quoting,
comments, Firefox version and unrelated settings are preserved. Unexpected
structure, aliases or conflicting selections require manual review, not broad
replacement. No local recipe or monitor state is modified in any mode.

Explicit proposal delivery (not activated here)
----------------------------------------------

Only --write enables external writes, and only in canonical/firefox-snap's
GitHub Actions context using its job-scoped GITHUB_TOKEN. The future workflow
must pass secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN with job-scoped contents: write and
pull-requests: write permissions. Do NOT pass a personal/App token, GH_TOKEN or
the existing repository-token secret: bot-token event suppression is what keeps
proposal pushes from invoking the push-triggered Launchpad importer. The command
does not fall back to those credentials. It rejects personal-token formats and,
before publishing a ref, verifies GitHub's default commit author/committer is
github-actions[bot]. Missing permissions or a different identity fail closed;
do not bypass this by explicitly supplying a bot author with a different token.

    python3 -B .github/scripts/check-rust-msrv.py --write

The command rereads nightly before preparing a proposal. If it changed during
archive lookup, the existing observation logic checks the newer recipe; a second
base check before ref creation stops a detected race. The immutable base tree
and recipe blob are checked, and the proposed commit inherits the complete base
tree with only snapcraft.yaml changed. The new ref is always
automation/rust-msrv-<full-required-version>, and the PR always targets
canonical/firefox-snap:nightly. Ref creation is atomic and never replaces an
existing branch. A later nightly update remains ordinary PR review/merge work;
this is not an atomic transaction across two GitHub refs.

Before delivery, all PR states/pages are inspected for the need identity or
proposal branch. An intact open bot proposal is reused without edits. Closed,
human-edited, retargeted or different-compiler proposals are held, not recreated
or overwritten. The PR contains durable identity, pinned source/snap evidence,
archive publications and content/head hashes in a machine-readable comment.
These hashes detect changes, not authenticate untrusted content. Duplicate
matches, corrupt metadata or an orphan branch stop delivery for manual recovery.
Ambiguous API failures are not automatically retried; the next invocation
reconciles existing work before attempting delivery. Broader lifecycle/resume,
report-Issue linkage and durable failure-state handling remain follow-up work.

No-write mode also inspects existing proposals and reports preview, existing or
held status without state persistence or external writes, even when a write-capable
token is present. A write-enabled run can report created or branch-created if PR
delivery subsequently fails. Unknown evidence/delivery returns status 1; held work
is an established observation, not an API failure. No mode writes to nightly,
beta, stable or ESR, merges/approves a PR, opens a report Issue, writes to Launchpad,
installs packages or runs builds. No workflow or automatic execution is installed
by this change. Maintainer permission/import-side-effect preflight and scheduled
activation remain separate rollout tasks; do not open live test PRs for validation.

Run the deterministic command-level tests without network access or credentials:

    python3 -B -m unittest discover -s .github/scripts/tests -v

The tests replace external HTTP I/O, reject filesystem mutations and all writes
in no-write mode, and exercise the real command and Debian version comparator.
Write-mode tests use disposable GitHub API records to inspect proposed blobs,
trees, refs and PRs without network requests or real credentials.
